    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 run / 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: when more than 80 owners have already been created today, the response carries `bulkSignupWarning: true`; otherwise `false`. The acceptance test reads it back via `GET /api/owners/{id}`, so it must be a field on the serialized `OwnerDto`.\n\nI kept structural impact low by adding one small cohesive unit and touching each existing function minimally:\n\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `bulkSignupWarning` boolean to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field through the already-proven serialization path (no wrapper/`@JsonUnwrapped` risk).\n- **`BulkSignup.java`** (new) \u2014 a tiny static util (one method, no surrounding class complexity) that counts owners whose `registrationDate` is the current business day and returns `count > 80`. It reuses `BusinessDay.roll` so it dates \"today\" exactly like `BuildOwner` and `RequireDailyCapacity` do.\n- **`RespondWithOwner`** (GET) and **`RespondWithOwnerCreated`** (POST) \u2014 each gained an `OwnerRepository` parameter and a single line setting the flag on the DTO before sending. Both are trivial one-method classes (WMC_other \u2248 0, CC 1), so the per-function cost stays minimal.\n\nThe `OwnerMapper` was left untouched: MapStruct's default `unmappedTargetPolicy` is `WARN`, so the extra read-only target compiles cleanly without an `ignore` mapping.",
